# Italian attaches its object pronouns to the end of a verb -- dammi, darli,
# prendila -- and reads a word that ends in one as the verb and the pronoun,
# which is what enclitics does: it asks whether what comes before the ending is
# one of Italian's verbs, and if so strips the ending and says it from a
# dictionary of its own. Polish has no such pronouns, and that dictionary is
# one of those emptied of Italian, so a Polish word that happens to spell an
# Italian verb and a pronoun lost most of itself: tonami and stanami, the
# plurals of ton and stan, came out as a third of a second of sound, and the
# phoneme report never came back from them at all.
#
# So Polish answers that no word ends in an enclitic. It is bare because it
# does nothing but answer, and the caller reads an answer that is not nought
# as the word being an ordinary one.
rule pol_no_enclitics takes 5 from it_morph.obj
  bare
  afresh
  answer 1
end

# Italian's prefixes are Italian's. strip_ital_prefixes reads bi off biały, sur
# off surowy and co off codziennie, and a seam there stops a letter rule
# seeing across it and costs a word its stress. Polish answers that no word
# begins with one of them, the same way it answers that none ends in an
# enclitic.
rule pol_no_prefixes takes 5 from it_morph.obj
  bare
  afresh
  answer 1
end
